a man who plotted to bomb New York City  subways could walk free from custody  within days Najibullah Zazi is being  rewarded for helping the government  identify and prosecute terrorists over  more than nine years he could have spent  the rest of his life behind bars for his  guilty plea on terrorism-related charges  but a federal judge sentence Ozzie to 10  years in prison yesterday and gave him  credit for time served  Jeff PJ's is in Washington Jeff good  morning good morning Najibullah Zazi  cooperation with US investigators earned  him his pending freedom prosecutors said  that his work as an informant has been  unprecedented and incredibly valuable  Najibullah Zazi was facing life in  prison for what authorities called one  of the most dangerous terrorist plots  since September 11 the 33 year old is a  naturalized US citizen and was  radicalized and trained by al-qaeda to  build explosives saasy and two friends  planned to detonate suicide bombs during  rush hour in New York City subways in  2009 there is no doubt that American  lives were saved then Attorney General  Eric Holder said the attack would have  been devastated after his arrest Ozzie  turned on al-qaeda he implicated his  friends and family even testifying  against one of them  Ozzie met with the government more than  100 times viewing hundreds of  photographs and providing critical  intelligence the government says Ozzy's  extraordinary assistance and insight  into al Qaeda and its members are still  helping with ongoing investigations  after Thursday's sentencing the judge  told Zazi this one unthinkable second  chance has come your way and you earned  it he's extremely appreciative and  thankful  for what happened today without question  za Z will be under supervised release or  federal probation for the rest of his  life he is expected to continue  cooperating with counterterrorism  investigations Nora  alright Jeff thank you
 

